Work that is understood well enough to write down but has not been done.
Entries are dated and say what was established, so that picking one up does not
mean starting the investigation over.

Not a backlog of ideas -- something belongs here once the shape of the problem
is known and the open questions are named.


================================================================================
Read p'(psi) and FF'(psi) from NetCDF, including MaNTA output
Raised 2026-08-24.
================================================================================

  Today `meq::SplineProfile` reads one format: a text table of
  `psi  f(psi)  f'(psi)` triples, via `fromFile()` / `fromStream()`
  (src/meq/Profiles.cpp). Configuration reaches it through `[source]` with
  `SourceType = "mhd"` and the `PPrimeFile` / `GGPrimeFile` keys.

  Wanted: the same two profiles read from a NetCDF file, which may or may not
  have been produced by MaNTA (../MaNTA). So there are really two readers, or
  one reader with a configurable name mapping.

  WHAT THE MaNTA FORMAT ACTUALLY IS

  Established by reading ../MaNTA/NetCDFIO.{hpp,cpp} and dumping
  ../MaNTA/python/Tests/JAXAuxTest.ref.nc. It uses the netCDF C++4 API
  (`#include <netcdf>`, netCDF::NcFile). Layout:

      dimensions:  t = UNLIMITED          time
                   x = <n>                space
      variables:   double t(t)            "Time since start of simulation", s
                   double x(x)            the spatial coordinate
                   double nVariables      count, at root
      group Grid:  CellBoundaries(Index), PolyOrder
      group VarN:  u(t,x)     "Value"
                   q(t,x)     "Derivative"
                   sigma(t,x) "Flux"

  So each variable is a group named `Var0`, `Var1`, ... and each carries BOTH
  the value and its derivative on the `x` grid. That is promising, because a
  Hermite cubic wants exactly a value and a derivative per knot -- `meq::Knot`
  is `{psi, value, derivative}`, which is the same triple.

  THE FOUR QUESTIONS TO SETTLE BEFORE WRITING ANY OF IT

  1. It is a derivative SHIFT, not a match. MaNTA gives (f, df/dx). meq's
     `MHDSource` wants p' and gg' as profiles OF psi, and calls `prime()` on
     them for the Newton Jacobian -- so it needs (p', p'') and (gg', gg'').
     If MaNTA's stored variable is p, then its (u, q) gives (p, dp/dx), which is
     one derivative short of what meq needs. Options, in rough order of
     preference:
       (a) have the MaNTA physics case output p' directly as its own variable,
           so u = p' and q = p'' and the triple lines up exactly;
       (b) read (p, dp/dx) and differentiate the spline once more -- loses an
           order and makes the Jacobian noisier than the residual, which is
           precisely the failure mode that shows up as Newton converging
           linearly rather than quadratically;
       (c) read only values and fit a spline, discarding MaNTA's derivatives.
     (a) is much the best if the MaNTA side can be arranged. Decide this first;
     it determines everything else.

  2. What is `x`, and how does it map to psi in [0,1]? meq's profiles are
     normalised to psi in [0,1] and `SplineProfile` clamps outside that. MaNTA's
     `x` is a radial coordinate whose meaning is set by the physics case, and
     nothing in the file says which. Needs an explicit config key naming the
     convention, plus the normalisation, rather than a guess. If x is not psi
     the chain rule lands on top of question 1.

  3. Which group is which? CORRECTED 2026-08-25 by MANTA-COUPLING.md, written
     from the MaNTA side: the premise below was out of date, and the reference
     file it was established from is one of the three exceptions.

     MaNTA physics cases name their own variables now, and the netCDF group
     takes that name -- so a modern case writes groups called `n`, `p_i`, `T_e`,
     each with a `description` attribute and `units` on every variable inside.
     `Var0`/`Var1` survive only for the three cases whose width comes from
     config and which therefore have no names to give (MatrixDiffusion,
     MatrixDiffusionTest, LinearDiffSourceTest) -- and JAXAuxTest.ref.nc, which
     this entry was written from, is one of them.

     A config key naming the mapping is still needed, since a group called `p_i`
     still has to be declared to be the pressure meq wants. But the information
     is there to match against rather than absent, and the `units` attribute is
     a free consistency check worth asserting on.

  4. Which time slice? MaNTA is time dependent and meq wants one equilibrium.
     Default to the last slice, `t` index size-1, but make it configurable and
     record the value of `t` that was used in meq's own output -- an equilibrium
     computed from an unrecorded time slice is not reproducible.

  THE NON-MaNTA CASE

  "May or may not have come from MaNTA" means there must be a flat convention
  too: 1-D variables over a single dimension, something like `psi`, `pprime`,
  `ffprime`, with derivatives optional. Detect the MaNTA layout by structure --
  the `Grid` group, or `nVariables` at root -- and fall back to the flat one.
  Prefer sniffing over a config flag, but make the choice overridable, and say
  in the log which was used.

  MECHANICS

  - `libnetcdf_c++4` is installed (/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libnetcdf_c++4.so)
    along with the C library. MaNTA links it, so the CMake pattern is there.
  - MFEM's own MFEM_USE_NETCDF is NO in ../mfem-hdg-dev, which does not matter:
    meq would link netcdf directly. The pre-modernisation Makefile did exactly
    that (`-lnetcdf_c++4 -lnetcdf`).
  - The old tree had a NetCDFIO.hpp, deleted in the restructure. It is not worth
    recovering -- it was a class declaration missing its terminating semicolon,
    so it had never been compiled. `git show v0-legacy:NetCDFIO.hpp` if curious.
  - Keep the reader out of `Profiles.{hpp,cpp}`. Those two units are
    deliberately free of every dependency including MFEM, which is what lets
    them be unit tested on their own. A `ProfilesNetCDF.{hpp,cpp}` returning a
    `SplineProfile` keeps that property.

  TESTS

  Write a small NetCDF file in the test itself rather than committing a fixture,
  so the expected values are visible next to the assertions. Cover: the MaNTA
  layout, the flat layout, a missing group, a missing variable, a time index out
  of range, and non-monotonic `x`. And check the round trip against the text
  reader -- the same profile through both paths must agree.


================================================================================
Sonic toroidal rotation
Raised 2026-08-25.
================================================================================

  meq solves the static Grad-Shafranov equation, whose source is

      F(r,z,psi) = mu0 r^2 dp/dpsi + g dg/dpsi.

  With rotation fast enough that the Mach number is order one -- "sonic" -- that
  is no longer the right equation. The centrifugal force sweeps heavy species to
  the outboard side, so the density is NOT a flux function, and an electrostatic
  potential arises to hold quasineutrality against that separation.

  The reference is ~/RoPP-final.pdf, Abel et al, "Multiscale gyrokinetics for
  rotating tokamak plasmas", Rep. Prog. Phys. 76 (2013) 116201. Its eq (136), on
  printed page 19, is the generalisation:

      Delta* psi = -4 pi R^2 sum_s n_s { T_s dlnN_s/dpsi
                        + [ Z_s e phi_0 - (1/2) m_s omega^2(psi) R^2 + T_s ]
                          dlnT_s/dpsi }
                   - 4 pi R^2 ( sum_s m_s n_s R^2 ) omega(psi) domega/dpsi
                   - I(psi) dI/dpsi

  coupled to eq (96) for the density,

      n_s = N_s(psi) exp[ m_s omega^2(psi) R^2 / (2 T_s) - Z_s e phi_0 / T_s ]

  and eq (97), the lowest-order quasineutrality condition that determines phi_0,

      sum_s Z_s N_s(psi) exp[ m_s omega^2(psi) R^2 / (2 T_s)
                              - Z_s e phi_0 / T_s ] = 0,

  subject to <phi_0>_psi = 0 by definition (their eq (59)).

  WHAT THIS CHANGES, in rough order of difficulty

  1. The profile set. Instead of two flux functions p'(psi) and gg'(psi), the
     source needs N_s(psi) and T_s(psi) PER SPECIES, plus omega(psi) and I(psi),
     and it needs dlnN_s/dpsi, dlnT_s/dpsi, domega/dpsi and dI/dpsi. meq::Profile
     already supplies value and derivative from one spline, so the interface is
     right; what changes is that Source stops being built from two profiles and
     becomes a small container of species.

  2. phi_0 is implicit. Eq (97) is a transcendental equation for phi_0 at each
     (R, psi) -- a scalar solve nested inside every source evaluation, i.e. at
     every quadrature point of every element on every Newton iteration.

     The good news is that this root find is as well behaved as it could be.
     Differentiating the left-hand side of (97),

         d/dphi_0 sum_s Z_s N_s exp[...] = -e sum_s (Z_s^2 / T_s) N_s exp[...],

     which is strictly negative for any positive N_s and T_s, since every term
     carries Z_s^2. So the left-hand side is strictly decreasing. And with at
     least one positive and one negative Z_s it runs from +infinity (phi_0 ->
     -infinity, ion terms dominate) to -infinity (phi_0 -> +infinity, electron
     terms dominate). Hence a unique root, always, and one that can be
     bracketed -- so a safeguarded Newton or Brent will not fail. A good initial
     guess is available from the previous iterate at the same point.

  3. NEWTON NEEDS d(phi_0)/d(psi), and it is not optional. dF/dpsi picks up phi_0
     through both the explicit Z_s e phi_0 term and the exponentials in n_s.
     Differentiate (97) implicitly with respect to psi at fixed R; the derivative
     falls out in closed form because (97) is differentiable in both arguments.
     Do NOT finite-difference it: an inner root find differenced from outside
     gives a Jacobian whose accuracy is set by the inner solve's tolerance,
     which is how a Newton iteration silently degrades to linear.

  4. <phi_0>_psi = 0 IS A GAUGE CHOICE, NOT A CLOSURE CONDITION, and it can be
     replaced by anything convenient. WITHDRAWN: an earlier version of this
     entry called it a non-local constraint over a moving flux surface and named
     it as possibly deciding the whole approach. That was wrong, and the
     correction matters because it removes the entry's one apparent blocker.

     The system has an exact gauge freedom. For any flux function delta(psi),

         phi_0 -> phi_0 + delta(psi),      N_s -> N_s exp( Z_s e delta / T_s )

     leaves the physical density n_s of (96) unchanged, and so leaves (97)
     satisfied. Only the split between "N_s" and "phi_0" moves; nothing
     observable does. <phi_0>_psi = 0 is the convention RoPP adopts, at its
     (59), to make N_s well defined -- which is why the paper says "by
     definition" rather than deriving it.

     AND THE SOURCE IS INVARIANT UNDER IT. Applying the transformation to the
     psi-derivative bracket of (136), the two changed terms are

         T_s dlnN_s/dpsi     picks up   Z_s e delta' - Z_s e delta T_s'/T_s
         [Z_s e phi_0 + ...] dlnT_s/dpsi   picks up   Z_s e delta T_s'/T_s

     which sum to Z_s e delta'. Weighted by n_s and summed over species that is
     e delta'(psi) sum_s Z_s n_s = 0, by quasineutrality -- i.e. by (97) itself.
     The remaining terms of (136) depend on phi_0 only through n_s, which is
     invariant by construction. Verified symbolically with sympy for a two
     species case, and the cancellation is exact rather than approximate.

     CONSEQUENCES, which are all simplifications:

       - Any normalisation gives the same equilibrium. phi_0(z = 0) = 0 on each
         flux surface is legitimate and is local to a curve rather than an
         integral over a surface.
       - Better still, no constraint is needed at solve time at all. Given
         N_s(psi) as input data, (97) already determines phi_0 uniquely and
         pointwise by item 2. The gauge choice is a statement about what the
         supplied N_s MEANS, not an equation the solver has to close.
       - So this must be documented where the profiles are documented: two sets
         of input N_s differing by the gauge factor describe the same plasma,
         and a user comparing profiles between codes needs to know which
         convention each is in. That is the only place it can still bite.

  CONVENTIONS TO PIN DOWN FIRST, because two sign or factor slips have already
  been found in the papers meq does follow

  - UNITS. RoPP is Gaussian -- the 4 pi in (136), the c in (135). meq and the
    Grad-Shafranov papers are SI with mu0. Convert deliberately and write the
    conversion down.
  - THE 2 pi. RoPP (31) has B = I grad(phi) + grad(psi) x grad(phi) with
    psi = A_phi, whereas HDG-GS-2 states psi = psi_p / 2 pi. The B decomposition
    agrees structurally -- their I is HDG-GS-2's g -- but the normalisation of
    psi does not obviously.
  - THE SIGN OF Delta*. RoPP (135) gives j . grad(phi) = -(c / 4 pi R^2) Delta*
    psi, so (136) reads Delta* psi = -4 pi R^2 {...}, i.e. -Delta* psi = +4 pi
    R^2 {...}. meq's convention is -Delta* psi = F. Check that maps to F = +4 pi
    R^2 {...} rather than assuming it.

  A CHEAP FIRST MILESTONE. The subsonic limit must reduce to what meq already
  solves: as omega -> 0 the exponentials go to one, n_s becomes a flux function
  again, and (136) should collapse to the static equation. Implement that limit
  first and check it against the existing Solov'ev benchmark to zero -- it
  exercises the whole new profile and phi_0 machinery while having a known
  answer.


================================================================================
Anisotropic pressure
Raised 2026-08-25.
================================================================================

  The other generalisation, and independent of the rotation one above: p_par is
  not equal to p_perp, so the scalar pressure p(psi) in the source is replaced
  by a pair, and the Grad-Shafranov equation changes shape rather than just
  gaining terms.

  NO REFERENCE IS PINNED FOR THIS YET, and one is needed before anything is
  written -- unlike the rotation case above, where ~/RoPP-final.pdf eq (136) is
  the equation to implement. The seeds below come from the CEDRES++ paper's own
  list of what it plans to add (refs/CEDRES.pdf, section 1 and its
  bibliography), so they are the set a production free-boundary code considered
  worth citing, not a literature search:

    Grad 1967                Toroidal containment of a plasma, Phys. Fluids 10, 137
    Maschke & Perrin 1984    An analytic solution of the stationary MHD equations
                             for a rotating toroidal plasma, Phys. Lett. A 102, 106
                             -- also relevant to the rotation entry above, and
                             worth having because an ANALYTIC solution is a
                             benchmark, which is the scarce thing here
    Goedbloed & Lifschitz 1997   Stationary symmetric MHD flows, Phys. Plasmas 4, 3544
    Zwingmann, Eriksson & Stubberfield 2001   Equilibrium analysis of tokamak
                             discharges with anisotropic pressure, PPCF 43, 1441
    Guazzotto, Betti, Manickam & Kaye 2004    Numerical study of tokamak
                             equilibria with arbitrary flow, Phys. Plasmas 11, 604
    Cooper et al 2009        Three-dimensional anisotropic pressure free boundary
                             equilibria, Comput. Phys. Commun. 180, 1524
    Pustovitov 2010          Anisotropic pressure effects on plasma equilibrium in
                             toroidal systems, PPCF 52, 065001
    Fitzgerald, Appel & Hole 2013   EFIT tokamak equilibria with toroidal flow and
                             anisotropic pressure using the two-temperature
                             guiding-centre plasma, Nucl. Fusion 53, 113408

  Verify each doi against Crossref before adding any of these to refs/Refs.md --
  they are transcribed from another paper's bibliography, which is exactly the
  provenance that file's opening rule exists for. This list has NOT been checked.

  Two things to establish when a reference is chosen:

  - Whether the anisotropy enters as a modified source with the same Delta*
    operator on the left, in which case meq's discretisation is untouched and
    only Source changes; or whether it changes the operator, in which case the
    HDG assembly changes too and this is a much larger job. Fitzgerald et al
    is the likely place to look, being an implementation.
  - Whether it composes with rotation. Fitzgerald's title suggests the two are
    treated together, which would make it the reference for both entries; if so
    the two should be planned as one piece of work rather than two.


================================================================================
Couple to MaNTA as a FieldModel
Raised 2026-08-25. See MANTA-COUPLING.md, which is the specification.
================================================================================

  A second, much tighter coupling than the NetCDF entry above: meq runs
  in-process as a plugin MaNTA dlopens, its Grad-Shafranov rows join MaNTA's
  DAE, and the equilibrium and the transport are solved together by one Newton
  rather than by an outer Picard loop.

  MANTA-COUPLING.md, written from the MaNTA side, is the contract and is far
  more detailed than this entry should duplicate. What belongs here is what meq
  must change, and the two things already established.

  THE ARCHITECTURAL DEMAND, which is not a detail

  MaNTA does not want a converged equilibrium. It wants the residual rows,
  evaluated at states that are nowhere near an equilibrium, on every residual
  evaluation, without complaint. meq's Newton is subsumed into MaNTA's.

  So GradShafranovSolver needs a residual-evaluation path beside its solve()
  path: given psi and a transport state, return R without solving anything.
  Stage 4 has most of the pieces -- prepare() already assembles and reduces
  without solving, and reducedOperator()/reducedRhs() already exist because the
  Jacobian check needed them. What is missing is the framing: a standalone
  solver needs to converge, a coupled one needs to be differentiable and
  evaluable OFF the solution manifold.

  The good news is that solveB, the operation MaNTA needs and will not accept a
  dense matrix for, is exactly meq's existing hybridized solve. That is the
  natural fit and the reason the trace space is the natural DOF vector.

  The two genuinely hard items, per that note's own checklist, are
  dGeometry_dpsi INCLUDING the moving-surface term -- a flux-surface average
  differentiated with respect to a psi that moves the surface -- and making the
  pointwise Geometry hook fast enough, since MaNTA calls it once per physics
  node per residual evaluation and hands over the whole psi vector each time.

  THE BUILD FLAGS, checked 2026-08-25, and narrower than the note fears

  MANTA-COUPLING.md warns that a plugin must be compiled with the flags
  `pkg-config --cflags manta` reports -- MaNTA's MANTA_ABI_FLAGS, currently
  `-DEIGEN_USE_BLAS -march=<concrete arch>`, znver2 on this machine -- because
  Eigen aligns to the widest vector unit the compiler knows about and inlines
  expression templates into both sides of the boundary. It flags reconciling
  this with meq's CMake and with how MFEM was built as an afternoon's work.

  It is smaller than that here, because NEITHER MFEM NOR meq USES EIGEN AT ALL
  (grepped, both). Eigen appears only where MaNTA's types cross the interface,
  which is the FieldModel subclass itself. So:

    - the plugin shim needs MaNTA's ABI flags;
    - meq_core and MFEM do not, and need not be rebuilt;
    - the boundary to keep Eigen on one side of is the shim, which is a
      handful of translation units rather than the whole library.

  The other trap in that section is a real one and costs nothing to respect:
  the plugin must NOT link -lmanta. MaNTA links its core objects directly, so a
  plugin pulling in libmanta.so gets a second copy of the registry and
  registers into a map the solver never reads, silently. Compile against the
  installed headers alone.

  BLOCKED ON THE MaNTA SIDE, so do not plan around them:

    - nScalars > 0 alongside a field model is refused today. A total-current
      constraint or a plasma-current control loop would need that unblocked.
    - DegreeAdaptation with a field model is refused.
    - An objective reading geometry directly loses its dG/dpsi term in the
      adjoint.

  AND FOUR QUESTIONS THAT NOTE LEAVES OPEN, two of which are ours to answer:

    - Who owns the p(psi) mapping, the MaNTA physics case or the meq field
      model? This decides which side owns which half of the A_2 chain rule.
      Ours to negotiate.
    - Should Geometry gain a batched form? Ours to ask for; the note suspects
      the pointwise-only signature is an oversight rather than a decision, and
      it is the hook that will hurt.
    - Whether the error test needs to exclude the field block, and whether
      nScalars needs unblocking first, are MaNTA-side.


================================================================================
More nonlinear test cases
Raised 2026-08-25. A survey, not yet a decision.
================================================================================

  meq's benchmark ladder is currently constant / linear / nonlinear in psi:
  Soloviev, McCarthy, and HDG-GS-1's Example 5. Only the last is nonlinear, and
  it is a manufactured solution rather than a physical equilibrium. What follows
  is what a search turned up, in the order it is worth pursuing.

  1. FOUR NONLINEAR SOURCES WE ALREADY OWN, in refs/HDG-GradShafranov-
     Adaptive.pdf sections 4.2 to 4.5. Fully specified, physically motivated,
     and stiff -- which is exactly what the Newton path has not yet been tested
     on. No exact solutions; the paper judges them by the error estimator, so
     they are stage-6 material for verification but usable as Newton stress
     tests now.

       4.2 pressure pedestal, eq (23)/(24):
           p = (c1 + c2 psi^2)(1 - exp(-(psi/sigma)^2))
           F = 2 r^2 psi ( c2(1 - exp(-(psi/sigma)^2))
                           + (1/sigma^2)(c1 + c2 psi^2) exp(-(psi/sigma)^2) )
           c1 = 0.8, c2 = 0.2, sigma^2 = 0.005, and g = const.
           sigma^2 = 0.005 makes the edge gradient very sharp. THIS is the case
           where Newton globalisation should start to matter -- see CLAUDE.md on
           KINSolver(KIN_LINESEARCH), which is exactly why that note exists.

       4.3 internal transport barrier, eq (25):
           p = [(1 + H erf(s(psi - psi_0)))/(1 + H)] (1 - (1 - psi)^a)^b
           H = 0.5, s = 40, psi_0 = 0.3, a = 4, b = 2.

       4.4 current hole, eq (26): the pedestal source plus
           c3 (1 - exp(-(psi/sigma_2)^2)) cos(c4 psi)
           c1 = 0.4, c2 = 0.1, c3 = -18, c4 = 10 pi,
           sigma_1^2 = 5e-3, sigma_2^2 = 3e-3.

       4.5 internal layer, eq (27): the pedestal source plus
           c3 (1 - exp(-(psi/sigma_1)^2)) exp(-(1 - r - psi)^2/sigma_2^2)
           c1 = 0.8, c2 = 0.2, c3 = 15, sigma_1^2 = 5e-3, sigma_2^2 = 7.5e-4.
           The paper is explicit that this one is NOT physical -- r appears
           inside the exponential, so it cannot be written as mu0 r^2 p' + g g'
           -- but it is a good test for detecting a localised internal layer.

     Note all four give dF/dpsi in closed form by differentiating the
     expressions above, so the Newton Jacobian stays exact.

  2. GENUINELY NONLINEAR EXACT SOLUTIONS: Kaltsas & Throumoulopoulos,
     Phys. Lett. A 380 (2016) 3373, doi 10.1016/j.physleta.2016.08.011,
     arXiv:1605.01538, OPEN ACCESS.

     Similarity reduction of the GS equation, giving closed forms for nonlinear
     free functions. The two that matter:

       f(u) = f0 + f1 u^2   -> a Weierstrass elliptic function (their eq 21)
       f(u) = f0 exp(n u)   -> sech^2 (their eq 22), plus a second class (23)

     The exponential/sech^2 case is the most attractive thing in this list: an
     EXACT solution, in elementary functions, of a genuinely nonlinear GS
     equation, with dF/dpsi analytic. Nothing else found offers that.

     Two caveats before building a benchmark on it. These are similarity
     solutions in a combined variable of the form (a r^2 +- 2 b z + const), so
     the domain is not free -- one has to find a closed level set to pose the
     problem on, exactly the difficulty the Solov'ev separatrix gave. And the
     paper says itself that for some of its reductions solutions "which possibly
     could result in closed magnetic surfaces are ruled out". Check that a
     usable closed contour exists BEFORE investing in the implementation.

  3. NOT NONLINEAR, despite appearances: Atanasiu, Gunter, Lackner & Miron,
     Phys. Plasmas 11 (2004) 3510, doi 10.1063/1.1756167. Two families of exact
     solutions, chosen so that "the GS equation becomes a LINEAR inhomogeneous
     differential equation" -- so this is McCarthy's rung, not a new one. Worth
     having anyway for a different reason: it constructs X-points by
     superposition and is explicitly intended as a benchmark near X-points,
     which is where stage 5's extension technique gives out. Two free parameters
     each in the pressure and poloidal current profiles.

  4. FOR CROSS-COMPARISON RATHER THAN AS TEST CASES:
       Pataki, Cerfon, Freidberg, Greengard & O'Neil, JCP 243 (2013) 28,
         doi 10.1016/j.jcp.2013.02.045 -- a fast high-order solver.
       Palha, Koren & Felici, JCP 316 (2016) 63, doi 10.1016/j.jcp.2016.04.002 --
         mimetic spectral elements; reports convergence for Soloviev, FRC and
         spheromak, so its tables are directly comparable with ours.
       VEQ, arXiv:2606.11821 (2026) -- fixed-boundary with flexible source
         profiles, and reports both a projected residual norm and a pointwise
         strong-form residual. That pair of diagnostics is worth stealing
         regardless of whether the solver is.

  All dois above are checked against Crossref. The arXiv ids are not.

  SUGGESTED ORDER. Do (1) first: it costs nothing but a Source subclass each,
  the parameters are all in a paper on disk, and the pedestal case tests the one
  thing Newton has not been pushed on. Then (2), if a closed contour exists.
  (3) and (4) are reading, not work.


================================================================================
Try PARDISO again against a real oneMKL build
Raised 2026-08-25. Measured, and blocked on a packaging defect rather than on
anything about the method.
================================================================================

  meq solves the hybridized trace system with mfem::UMFPackSolver and
  UMFPACK_ORDERING_METIS (src/meq/GradShafranov.cpp, both the linear and the
  Newton paths). PARDISO was measured as an alternative and is faster where it
  runs -- and it stops running just below the sizes meq uses.

  WHAT WAS MEASURED, on meq's own trace matrix at k = 3:

      n        UMFPACK total   PARDISO total   numeric factorisation   agreement
        832        2.8 ms          1.9 ms        2.0 -> 0.7 ms          2e-15
      3,200       18.9 ms          8.6 ms       13.9 -> 2.1 ms          6e-15
      7,104       44.1 ms       error -3            --                    --
     12,544       90.9 ms       error -3            --                    --

  2.2x overall and 6.6x on the factorisation, agreeing with UMFPACK to 1e-15 --
  and then error -3 (reordering) above n of about 3000.

  THE FAILURE IS DEBIAN'S intel-mkl 2020.4.304, NOT THE CALLING CODE. Shown on
  a plain 5-point Laplacian with no MFEM linked at all: n = 1600 succeeds,
  n = 12544 fails with the same -3, and of the three reorderings only
  iparm[1] = 3 (parallel nested dissection) survives at that size, while 0
  (minimum degree) and 2 (serial METIS) both fail. The 1e-15 agreement at small
  n is what separates "the calls are wrong" from "the library is broken".

  Three other hypotheses were tested and are all dead, so do not re-test them:
  a METIS symbol collision with MFEM's -lmetis (adding it to a working build
  changes nothing, and libmkl_core does not export those symbols); an
  interaction with MFEM's link line (the minimal test runs fine on meq's
  complete link line); and something particular to meq's matrix (the plain
  Laplacian fails identically).

  WHAT A PROPER ATTEMPT LOOKS LIKE

  1. Install current Intel oneAPI MKL -- its own installer or the
     intel-oneapi-mkl apt repository. NOT Debian's intel-mkl.
  2. Rebuild ../mfem/install with MFEM_USE_MKL_PARDISO=YES, which wants
     MKL_PARDISO_OPT and MKL_PARDISO_LIB. Watch for meq's existing MKL
     BLAS/LAPACK link line straddling two MKL versions; that is the first thing
     likely to go wrong.
  3. Re-run at n = 12544 and n = 49664, the two sizes that fail today. If they
     pass, the defect is confirmed as packaging and the table above becomes the
     operative one.
  4. Use MFEM's own PardisoSolver (linalg/pardiso.hpp, behind
     MFEM_USE_MKL_PARDISO) rather than hand-rolled PARDISO() calls. Note INSTALL
     documents only MFEM_USE_MKL_CPARDISO, the cluster version, so the serial
     wrapper is easy to miss.

  SETTLE THE REPRODUCIBILITY QUESTION FIRST, because it can kill this even if
  oneMKL works. PARDISO's advantage IS the threading, and threaded reduction
  order is the same non-determinism that decides the pedestal knife edge -- see
  CLAUDE.md under "On SUNDIALS", where MFEM_USE_LAPACK tips a marginal Newton
  by changing rounding and nothing else. This suite asserts Newton iteration
  counts. iparm[33], conditional numerical reproducibility, exists for exactly
  this; it costs some of the speed back and the question is how much. Measure
  it, do not assume it.

  AND IT IS SECOND IN LINE. The symbolic factorisation is 22-24% of every
  factorisation and UMFPackSolver::SetOperator throws it away and redoes it on
  every Newton step, on a sparsity pattern that never changes. That fix works on
  today's library, needs no new dependency, and is planned in
  ../mfem-hdg-dev/doc/DIRECT-SOLVER-SYMBOLIC-REUSE.md. Do that first; PARDISO is
  the speculative upside behind it.


================================================================================
Performance: profile first, then decide which of MFEM's backends can apply
Raised 2026-08-26. The goal is to be fast enough that mesh resolution and
polynomial degree are chosen for accuracy and not for patience.
================================================================================

  NOTHING HAS EVER BEEN PROFILED. Every number below is a structural reading of
  the build or a measurement taken for some other purpose, and the first task is
  to replace them with a profile of a representative solve. Choosing a backend
  before knowing where the time goes is how a week disappears into a 3% win.

  WHAT IS ALREADY KNOWN, from measurements taken for other reasons:

  1. The global trace solve. UMFPACK with METIS ordering, measured at 91 ms for
     n = 12544 and 443 ms for n = 49664 (k = 3). Of every factorisation, 22-24%
     is the SYMBOLIC phase, which UMFPackSolver::SetOperator recomputes on every
     Newton step for a sparsity pattern that never changes. Planned in
     ../mfem-hdg-dev/doc/DIRECT-SOLVER-SYMBOLIC-REUSE.md. Roughly a quarter of
     the linear cost of a Newton solve, and the single cheapest win identified.

  2. The element-local solves, which dominate the STIFF cases rather than the
     easy ones. On section 4.2 at k = 1, h = 0.05 the outer iteration count went
     42 -> 36 -> 25 for LSsolveType Newton -> LBFGS -> LBB, each outer iteration
     carrying one local solve per element. setLocalSolver() exposes the choice;
     which is fastest has been measured only on that one case.

  3. PARDISO is 2.2x faster than UMFPACK on meq's own matrices where it runs,
     and does not run above n ~ 3000 on this machine's MKL. Its own TODO entry
     above.

  WHAT MFEM'S PERFORMANCE BACKENDS CAN AND CANNOT DO HERE, and the answer is
  mostly "not yet", which is worth knowing before anyone gets excited:

  * PARTIAL ASSEMBLY IS NOT IMPLEMENTED FOR THE HDG INTEGRATORS. DarcyForm has
    SetAssemblyLevel() and forwards it to every sub-form, so the interface is
    there -- but fem/darcy/bilininteg_hdg.cpp and darcyhybridization.cpp contain
    ZERO AssemblePA or AddMultPA methods. HDGDiffusionIntegrator,
    NormalTraceJumpIntegrator and the rest are FULL-assembly only. Asking for
    AssemblyLevel::PARTIAL today gets an unimplemented path, not a fast one.
    Writing those kernels is real work and belongs upstream, not in meq.

  * PARTIAL ASSEMBLY WANTS TENSOR-PRODUCT ELEMENTS AND meq USES TRIANGLES. The
    performance case for PA is sum factorisation, which needs quads in 2D. meq
    meshes are triangles throughout -- Mesh::MakeCartesian2D with
    Element::TRIANGLE -- because that is what the GS papers use and what the
    extension technique's transfer paths were measured on. A serious PA effort
    would have to answer the quad question first, and that is a discretisation
    decision with its own convergence consequences, not a build flag.

  * THE INSTALL HAS NO DEVICE BACKEND. ../mfem/install is built with
    MFEM_USE_CUDA, RAJA, OCCA, CEED, OPENMP and SIMD all NO. Enabling them is a
    rebuild, but see the two points above: without PA kernels for the HDG
    integrators there is nothing for a device to run, and mfem::Device would
    accelerate the parts of the assembly that are not the bottleneck.

  * meq IS SERIAL BY DESIGN. MFEM_USE_MPI = NO, and CLAUDE.md records why. A
    parallel meq is a different project with its own tree (../mfem-hdg-dev-par);
    do not conflate "fast" with "parallel" here.

  SO THE ORDER IS:

  1. Profile a representative solve -- perf, or MFEM_USE_CALIPER which is a
     build flag away. Get the split between assembly, the global factorisation,
     the local solves and the reconstruction. Everything below is provisional
     until this exists.
  2. Take the symbolic reuse, which is planned and needs no new dependency.
  3. Measure LSsolveType properly across the benchmark suite rather than on one
     case, and set the default from that.
  4. Only then ask whether PA and a device are worth the discretisation change
     they would demand.

  AND A CHEAP CHECK FIRST: confirm the release build is what everyone thinks it
  is. meq builds -O3 -DNDEBUG; MFEM's install carries MFEM_USE_MEMALLOC = YES,
  whose stack-based allocator is a help or a hindrance depending on the access
  pattern and has never been measured either way here.
